//! run
//! abort
//! stderr: ft:24:29: runtime error: out of memory
//! stdout:
//| before
// The runtime never gives the address range of a released allocation to a later allocation (D10.9
// rule 1). So a loop that allocates and releases 4 GiB comes to the end of the address space of
// the process, and the `new` there is the runtime error `out of memory` (rule 5). The loop
// writes the last byte of the first 4 allocations only, so the run needs little storage. The
// program prints no count: the size of the address space is a property of the system. The
// address space of each target has at most 2^47 bytes, which is 32768 allocations of 4 GiB, so
// the loop stops after 65536 rounds: a run that gets to the end prints a line that the test
// does not expect, and it ends in a bounded time.
// D10.2, D10.6, D10.9
fn size() u64 {
    return 4294967296;
}

fn main() i32 {
    println("before");
    u64 n = size();
    u64 mut sum = 0;
    for (u64 mut i = 0; i < 65536; i += 1) {
        u8 mut@ own block = new(u8, n);
        if (i < 4) {
            block[n - 1] = 1;
            sum += cast(block[n - 1], u64);
        }
        del(block);
    }
    println("after ", sum);
    return 0;
}
